I read polls about religious belief with  great interest and in many of these  polls the following question is asked do  you believe in God most of the time the  responses are broken down into yes/no or  don't know naathan seems to me that the  first thing one should do when asked by  a pollster if he or she believes in God  is to ask the questioner what do you  mean by God if you tell me what you mean  I'll tell you whether I believe the  reason for this is that there are so  many conceptions of God that grouping  all of the yes answers into one category  doesn't tell us much about what those  people actually believe or what the  people who answer know are actually  denying there's a wide range of  conceptions of God and we need to  explore some of the most commonly held  beliefs in almost every tradition we're  going to study we're going to encounter  various supernatural beings God God's  spirits so on the category of divinity  is one of the five key themes that will  encounter in almost every religious  tradition and the purpose of this  lecture is to examine each of these  categories in addition to divinity the  other four categories are scripture  ritual good and evil and soteriology or  the doctrine of salvation or liberation  will gain some general knowledge about  these important aspects of religion  which will help prepare us for our study  the world's religions  why not begin our study of religious  themes thinking about the biggest topic  of all God but before we even begin we  have a problem with language perhaps  it's not even a good idea to just use  the word God to describe all the views  we'll discuss is that word tends to  invoke the God of the Bible or Quran  which might be misleading when talking  about the gods of non monotheistic  religions some scholars use more general  terms such as divinity or ultimate  reality to refer to the realm of being  that is beyond the human and of supreme  importance people who believe in some  kind of God or gods are called theists  after the word fails from the Greek for  God that's where we get the term  theology a discourse or understanding  about God that words related to the  Latin deus from which we get words like  deity and divine those who believe that  there's no God are atheists and  agnostics are those who do not know  whether there's a God or not Gnostics  believe that if we do not or cannot have  knowledge about God's existence the only  responsible position is I don't know  some scholars have argued that simply  asking people whether or not they  believe in God isn't sufficient to  really capture the attitude people have  towards the existence of a deity for one  thing it does not tell us how they  conceive of God and second it doesn't  tell us how confidently they hold the  belief Richard Dawkins came up with a  seven-point scale to determine people's  believes with more precision try to  locate where you are on this scale  category 1 absolute certainty in the  existence of God to high degree of  confidence in the existence of God but  with some doubt 3 leaning toward belief  in existence but with less confidence 4  completely neutral there's an equal  degree of probability that there is or  is not a God 5 leaning toward rejection  of the belief in God 6 high degree of  confidence that God does not exist but  with some doubt seven absolute certainty  in the non-existence of God now simply  asking people the yes/no question do you  believe in God it makes it seem like  there's only ones and sevens out there  however in the many years that I've been  asking students to locate themselves on  this scale I find that the majority fall  in categories two through six  many people have at least some doubts  and after that it's a matter of degree  so rather than ask yourself whether or  not you believe in God ask yourself  where on the scale you would be when we  turn to conceptions of God we find a  number of categories that determine the  nature of people's beliefs so first the  number of God's how many are there  according to the latest statistics over  half of the world's population believes  in one God it's proximately 55% it's a  very rough estimate in other words they  are mono theists monotheists believe in  one Supreme Being and emphasize the  unity of that being although there are  doctrines like the Trinity that believe  in different persons or aspects within  the one God the reason for the high  percentage of monotheists is the large  number of Christians and Muslims who  together make up about half the world's  population along with smaller numbers of  Jews Sikhs the highs and other  monotheists but at the same time there  are more religious traditions that are  polytheistic meaning a belief in many  gods from Chinese folk religions and  Shinto in East Asia to numerous  indigenous traditions in Africa  Australia and Central and South America  many religions of antiquity including  the classical Greek and Roman religions  as well as the Celtic were polytheistic  there are many varieties of polytheism  normally the gods are organized into  Pantheon's although this may be done in  different ways in some cases each God  occupies a different plane of existence  or has a different function and these  deities do not interact with each other  much but in most cases the gods do  interact with each other often making  alliances or fighting marrying or  engaging in other  activities that parallel those of the  human realm in some way in some cases  the gods arranged in a hierarchy in  Chinese folk religion as we'll see the  gods staff a cosmic bureaucracy in some  cultures individuals or communities  worship multiple gods but in other cases  people worship one God but recognize the  existence of other gods another belief  system is pantheism which is derived  from the root pan or all and that's the  belief that God is identical with the  universe or at least the natural  universe the natural world is itself  divine some forms of Taoism can be seen  this way for example monotheism's  emphasis on oneness normally takes the  form of a single being who creates a  world like a craftsman creates an object  so the creators related to the world but  it's not identical with it but  pantheists see all of reality is one  there's no duality between the divine  and the natural world in some of his  writings Albert Einstein who described  himself as an agnostic leans in this  direction while he rejected a belief in  a personal God he wrote quote if  something is in me which can be called  religious then it is the unbounded  admiration for the structure of the  world so far as our science can reveal  it finally there are traditions such as  n Buddhism that do not feature God gods  or supernatural beings rather than  focusing on external beings with whom we  can relate such traditions emphasize  uncovering the spiritual possibilities  within ourselves the next question that  can be asked is what form if any does  God or the gods take in some traditions  there is the emphasis on a formless God  is there's a concern that any attempt to  picture or represent God limits an  infinite being and leads to idolatry the  worship of the image instead of God  however some believe that it's difficult  to have a personal relationship with an  abstract formless impersonal deity for  them a God with form is easier to relate  to  some traditions also teach that  directing one's worship toward a  representation can help to focus one on  the divine when deities are understood  to possess form they're quite often seen  as being similar to human beings and  this is known as anthropomorphism which  means attributing human form and  characteristics to the deities gods are  portrayed as having basically human-like  bodies but often with variations like  more arms and legs greater stature and  power abilities humans don't have  immortality so on  the gods are also seen as possessing  human emotions such as anger compassion  love and jealousy what particular kinds  of images do people have of God when  people talk about God and are searching  for ways to describe a being that's  often understood is infinite  transcendent and ineffable they have no  choice but to look for metaphors I mean  all we know is our human world  so believers need to think of God and  their relationship with God in terms  they can understand so think about the  various images of God that people have  beginning with God as a parent if God  has created us loves us guides us and  watches over us what better metaphor is  there than a parental one one issue that  arises with the parental metaphor  involves gender in traditional  monotheism God is frequently described  as Father but if this language is just  metaphorical and God doesn't really have  a gender then why not also refer to God  as mother aren't many of God's  characteristics reflective of a mother's  care as much as a father's if when we  picture God we picture a man what does  that mean for how we'll view men and  women within religious traditions  another common image is God as ruler  Lord or judge these images emphasize  God's authority in God's activity of  dispensing justice but in many  traditions people have metaphors that  move away from focusing on God's power  and authority and instead focuses on  God's love and compassion  here we see conceptions of God as friend  or in some cases as our beloved we'll  see religious poetry that uses romantic  passionate imagery to talk about the  longing of the soul for union with God  and in some traditions God can be  portrayed as a child or even a baby  images of innocence purity and even  playfulness can be seen in some  traditions on the other side of the  spectrum God can sometimes be seen as a  destroyer a being whose wrathful and  bloodthirsty in some traditions gods are  seen as having the forms of animals or  human animal hybrids in other cases the  natural elements themselves the Sun  stars or fire can themselves be seen as  deities rather than think of a  human-like deity associated with the  element who controls the element the  natural element or object itself is  worshipped we'll see examples of all of  these in the coming lectures  we now move to our second category  scripture I'd like you to think of the  following situation you arrive in a  place you've never been experiencing a  culture you know absolutely nothing  about the language they speak has no  general word for scripture how would you  find out what their scriptures are if  they have any if you knew the language  could you find the texts or texts that  could be considered scripture too many  or all people within that culture I mean  what features would it have to have  would it consists of stories laws and  rules poetry philosophy history in the  religions we're going to study we'll see  examples of all of these genres and many  more and of course there are plenty of  non scriptural books that contain these  genres so if there's nothing  specifically about the content of a book  that can tell us if it's a scripture  what can i would argue that to determine  which book the culture considers  scripture you have to watch the way that  practitioners of that religion relate to  certain books what will people do in  relationship to a scripture well as  we'll see they often chanted recited in  community rituals cover it with  decorative adornment memorize it compose  commentaries about it write it in  beautiful calligraphy give lectures and  sermons about it  put it in a special location but most  importantly it will be used as an  authority and people in the culture will  understand its words to contain  important truths and will attempt to  guide their lives by it recognized  authority figures like priests scholars  theologians and jurists will attempt to  settle disputes set up legal codes  organize institutions and communities  and provide guidance and wisdom by  consulting the text in other words the  status of Scripture comes to a text not  simply through its own features but  through a relationship between the text  and a community and we must ask another  question about these sacred words how  are they to be understood what do they  mean how do we interpret  texts and who has the authority to judge  the truth and validity of  interpretations this will be a central  question that arises in every tradition  very few people treat every passage of a  scripture is equally important with  other passages which passages are  emphasized by particular communities or  individuals should passages be  interpreted literally metaphorically  with the help of other texts what  scholarly method should be applied to  scriptures so these are questions that  religious people of all traditions  struggle with  we now move to our third category ritual  while some traditions might lack a  conception of God or a written scripture  all of them feature rituals when we  think about our lives we can see that  they're structured in numerous ways by  ritual activity from our daily rituals  to important rituals at certain moments  in our lives to large-scale rituals  carried out by a religious institution  or the state but what exactly is a  ritual and how does it differ from a  habit or a routine here's one way to  think about it as opposed to a habit or  routine ritual is characterized by  symbolic action certain body movements  and verbal expressions often accompanied  by the use of certain objects but are  not just practical ways to get something  done so on this understanding we would  not say that brushing our teeth is a  ritual just because we do it every day  but ritual can encompass those small  gestures and words that characterize our  day-to-day interactions insofar as they  constitute agreed on patterns and forms  that we carry out in our communities so  these would include shaking hands or  bowing or giving a toast at dinner in a  religious context rituals can be  understood as the formalized movements  and language that are given to us by our  traditions  and are carried out in contexts that are  understood as sacred rituals can be  divided into a number of categories one  type of ritual is the life cycle ritual  these are often called rites of passage  as they mark a transition from one state  to the next  the major passages that are covered in  almost all traditions are birth  coming-of-age marriage and death events  like birth and death are biological  events of course but there's also a  profoundly important cultural dimension  a new baby becomes a member of a  particular religious community for  example through a ritual such as baptism  or circumcision or a naming ceremony  a death is marked by a ritual that  enables a transition both for the  deceased and for the survivors in the  cases of coming-of-age and wedding there  is not a single biological event that's  given cultural meaning by the ritual  rather the ritual itself is what brings  about the change in status in the eyes  of a religious community a couple is  married only when the proper ritual is  completed so the proper series of  gestures and words can in fact bring  about a new reality this is the power of  ritual another category is nature based  rituals and these are cyclic seasonal  celebrations such rituals connect human  beings up with the movements of the  larger natural world cultivating a sense  of gratitude and reverence for nature  and in some cases the god or gods who  create and sustain the natural world the  most common of these are the rituals  that occur during solstices and  equinoxes these are important rituals in  nature based traditions from the pagan  traditions that predate Christianity to  later traditions that drawn them such as  Wicca but they can also be seen in  monotheistic traditions that often  incorporate these seasonal rituals into  their own historically based rituals  that's why Passover an Easter shares so  many elements with spring rituals and  Christmas with the winter solstice this  brings us to historically based rituals  these are rituals that commemorate  important events in the history of a  tradition in many cases this might be an  event related to the founder of the  tradition such as the birth or death  these kinds of rituals are important in  Christianity and Buddhism for instance  historically based rituals can also  involve pivotal events in the formation  of the community or tradition such as  the Jewish Exodus commemorated and  Passover the revelation of the Quran to  Muhammad commemorated during Ramadan or  the enlightenment of the Buddha marked  during Bodhi Day rituals can infuse our  ordinary lives with a sense of the  sacred reflect on the ways that our  shared meals are transformed through  ritual from the bile  logical act of eating to a cultural  expression of togetherness  think of how ritual guides the ways we  express respect gratitude or remorse to  each other or help us navigate the act  of meeting a stranger as we look at  rituals in the world's religious  traditions and the lectures ahead we'll  see that they serve many functions and  accomplish many ends they connect human  beings with ultimate reality as they  conceive it that's what we might call a  vertical connection and they connect  people with each other in communities  it's a horizontal connection they also  connect people with their tradition and  its history educating them about the  beliefs and values of the tradition and  commemorating historical events rituals  enable human beings to navigate  difficult transitions such as the death  of a loved one and bring about new  realities such as the change in status  of a child to adult single to married  layperson to priests ritual cultivates  and expresses feelings attitudes and  worldviews  I mean think of the way that music art  and architecture the stylized movement  of bodies and special clothing the  reciting of prayers and sacred texts can  put you in a certain mood and frame of  mind can awaken powerful feelings in you  or make you see the world in a different  way  we now enter the realm of good and evil  religious traditions have to account for  why their suffering and evil in the  world and offer a way to understand and  cope with it it's during difficult times  that many people are likely to turn  toward religion as they seek comfort and  meaning with all of the profound  differences that exist among religious  traditions they all seem to share at  least one thing a belief that there's  some basic order in the cosmos it's hard  to imagine a religious tradition that  would state that everything simply  random that there's no justice no larger  pattern no meaning and yet when we look  at the world it certainly seems that  there's some truth in the following news  story from the satirical newspaper the  onion with its headline life unfair for  the 50 billions consecutive weeks since  its inception life was revealed to be  unfair Monday death and suffering  continue to be dispersed randomly among  the planets life forms with such  potentially mitigating factors as solid  community standing genetic superiority  and previous good works in no way taken  into account the articles conclusion  life is expected to remain unfair far  into the foreseeable future  well religious traditions have to find a  compelling way to counter this claim  monotheistic traditions have a challenge  in doing so if God is omniscient or  all-knowing omnipotent all-powerful and  omnibot Evelynn all good why is there  evil in the world the attempt to solve  this problem is called theodicy which  comes from the greek meaning the Justice  of God since God is all-powerful God  can't be unable to end evil and  suffering and since God's all good  he shouldn't be unwilling to end it so  what kind of theodicies are possible  well one theodicy is based on freewill  arguing that God created people with the  freedom to choose good or evil which  means that some will choose evil and  harm others the alternative would have  been to create people unable to choose  evil but then we would be puppets or  automatons not free beings  one of the problems with this attempt is  that it only works to explain the evil  human beings do to each other and not  the suffering caused by natural events  like floods hurricanes famines and  disease other theodicies emphasize that  we grow as people and become strong  through suffering that a full meaningful  life requires struggle and we can't know  joy without sorrow some call this the  soul making theodicy this might be  effective in some context but it's hard  to see how it would apply in a situation  where a child dies and this never has a  chance to grow and learn from the  experience some theodicies advised us to  simply accept that it's all a part of  God's plan that everything will be made  alright in the end  this might mean that we receive rewards  and punishments after each of us dies so  that any theming in justice is rectified  it also might be connected with  eschatology theories about the end times  where the balancing out happens in a new  age or Kingdom some theologians have  been unsatisfied with these attempts to  make sense of evil and suffering and so  they've come to understand God  differently perhaps God is not  omnipotent after all perhaps God is not  the kind of being who can directly  intervene in human affairs maybe God  only acts in cooperation with or through  human beings providing a source of power  from whom human beings can derive  strength for some feel Logan's God is  not the one who can prevent suffering  God is the one who suffers with us God  cannot prevent bad things from happening  but can be a source of solace when they  do it's also important to note the  traditions that are not theistic also  have to come up with theodicies and  these also have their weaknesses as  we'll see many Asian traditions feature  a belief in karma and rebirth the law of  cause and effect where our actions good  and bad lead to good and bad results  later in this life or in a future life  these systems can account for seemingly  unjust suffering by seeing it  as a result of past Karma perhaps from a  previous life but some think thinkers  have critiqued this way of thinking by  arguing that it seems to say that  whatever it happens to one is deserved  in some way like a way of blaming the  victim many forms of karma theory avoid  this result but inevitably all Fiat is's  run into difficulties struggle with evil  and suffering it's one of the most  profound challenges that all religious  traditions have to face religious  traditions are not only concerned with  explaining and addressing the problem of  evil but also with the pursuit of the  good ethical questions include how  should I live what is good and bad right  and wrong one version of religious  ethics states simply that we know what's  moral by looking at God's commandments  but this raises many problems such as  how do we interpret and apply these  Commandments and what happens when what  appears to be a command conflicts with  our conscience just imagine being in  abraham's position and being commanded  to kill your child many forms of ethics  focus on the rightness or wrongness of  actions but ethical judgment can also  focus on people such forms of ethics  emphasize character cultivation and the  development of virtues and are therefore  known as character or virtue ethics  classical Greek and Chinese ethics are  of this kind and both center on the most  important virtues and how to cultivate  them in other words instead of focusing  on individual actions you work to become  a good person with guidance from your  tradition and then good actions will  flow from your character so if you're a  compassionate generous fair person then  you will act in these ways without  having to calculate the right thing to  do in each situation virtually every  religious tradition has an element of  virtue ethics and it's instructive to  compare the virtues that are considered  the most important in each tradition  think about the cardinal virtues in the  traditions of classical Greek thought  wisdom justice courage and temperance  Confucianism benevolence propriety  righteousness and wisdom Buddhism  compassion loving kindness sympathetic  joy and equanimity and Christianity  faith hope and charity or unconditional  love we can also compare the various  methods used to cultivate virtuous  characters some traditions advocate  learning some rituals some meditation as  an exercise  think about what set of virtues and try  to limit yourself to four are the most  important for human beings to possess  and what are the most effective methods  for cultivating them as we look at the  various religious traditions  we'll keep thinking about these  questions  we now arrive at our final topic for  this lecture soteriology this word comes  from the Greek word so ter which means  Savior  so soteriology means the doctrine of  salvation or liberation as we've just  discussed religious traditions are guide  to living a good life to understanding  right and wrong virtues and vices but  they're more than that at the highest  level they promise to deliver us from a  profoundly unsatisfactory state to an  infinitely better one which is  understood by terms like salvation  liberation enlightenment for example the  beginning states can be described as sin  a delusion or alienation the final state  as paradise heaven being saved nirvana  and so on but amidst all these  differences we can see a structural  similarity among the traditions  theologian John hick puts it this way  religions all provide a way to move  beyond the initial state which is often  seen as some kind of self-centered state  one of dissatisfaction to the final  state in which one experiences a  profound connection to the ultimate  reality and achieves the highest  spiritual state the path from the  initial to the final state can be  understood as the soteriology the  doctrine of how to be liberated or saved  which includes an understanding of what  that final state is like when we think  about this path and the various  religions we studied we can ask can we  achieve the highest state through our  own efforts or do we need the  intervention of some power beyond us as  we look at the different traditions  we'll examine their diagnosis of what  afflicts us how it can be overcome what  the ultimate goal is and how to achieve  it  and with these questions in mind we now  turn to South Asia and the first  religious tradition we will study  Hinduism
 

